{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>אֶת דִּמְכֶם לְנַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם</b> — your blood, which is your souls, that is to say, \"your blood which is your souls,\" in the manner of \"לְכֹל כְּלֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן\" (Exodus 27:19), and similarly \"הַשְּׁלִישִׁי לְאַבְשָׁלוֹם\" (I Chronicles 3:2). And one may interpret \"אֶת דִּמְכֶם לְנַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם\" as \"in your souls,\" for \"the life of all flesh, its blood is in its soul\" (Leviticus 17:14), and similar to it, \"וְכָל דָּם לֹא תֹאכְלוּ בְּכֹל מוֹשְׁבֹתֵיכֶם לָעוֹף וְלַבְּהֵמָה\" (Leviticus 7:26) — its solution is referring to the fowl or the beast. But the correct interpretation is that He says: \"the blood which is for the soul, I will demand from among you\" — He indicated that the blood is the soul, and He hinted that for one who sheds the soul He imposes the death penalty, but not for the blood of the limbs in which the life-force is not suspended. And our Rabbis (Bava Kamma 91a) expounded from it concerning one who kills himself: \"אֶת דִּמְכֶם — מִנַּפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם — אֶדְרֹשׁ\" — I will demand it." "<b>מִיַּד כָּל חַיָּה אֶדְרְשֶׁנּוּ</b> — I am astonished if the demand is according to its plain sense, from the hand of the beast just as from the hand of the man, so that there should be punishment for it, since a beast has no understanding to be punished or to receive reward. But perhaps this is so specifically regarding the blood of a man alone, that any beast that tears him apart shall be torn apart, for it is a decree of the King. And this is the reason for \"the ox shall be surely stoned, and its flesh shall not be eaten\" (Exodus 21:28) — and it is not in order to punish its owner monetarily, for even an ownerless ox is liable to death (Bava Kamma 44a), and He so commanded concerning the children of Noah just as concerning Israel. And the meaning of \"one who sheds the blood of a man\" would be: every shedder, whether beast or man, and his blood is demanded both in the court and by the hands of Heaven. And it is possible that the meaning of \"מִיַּד כָּל חַיָּה\" is that the vengeance upon the one who sheds blood shall come by the hand of every beast, in the manner of \"for she has received from the hand of YHWH double for all her sins\" (Isaiah 40:2), and He said: \"only your blood I will demand, and I will avenge it by the hand of every beast,\" for I will send against the murderer every beast of the earth, and I will also send a man against him, and he will not escape from their hands. And similar to it: \"מִכָּל צֹרְרַי הָיִיתִי חֶרְפָּה\" (Psalms 31:12), from their hands; \"זֶה חֵלֶק אָדָם רָשָׁע מֵאֱלֹהִים וְנַחֲלַת אִמְרוֹ מֵאֵל\" (Job 20:29). Or perhaps the demand from the hand of the beast is that it shall not tear a man apart, for so He has set in their nature. And the secret of the matter is that at the time of formation He gave to man every herb yielding seed and every tree in which is the fruit of a tree for food, and He gave to the beast every green herb for food, and Scripture says (Genesis 1:30), \"וַיְהִי כֵן\" — for that is their nature and their way. And now, when He said concerning man that he may slaughter living creatures, and it was established in nature or in practice that living creatures would be food for one another, it was necessary to command that the rest of the living creatures should be prey for the teeth of the sons of men, and that they would fear them and not tear them apart. And He said, \"וְאַךְ אֶת דִּמְכֶם לְנַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם,\" to hint that He will not demand the blood of a beast from the hand of its fellow beast, for if so, it would remain in them to tear one another apart. And this is the reason He mentioned the prohibition of bloodshed here — because of the permission of slaughter, for the practice would now be to shed blood. For according to the opinion of our Rabbis (Sanhedrin 56b), the first man had already been warned concerning this, but because of the slaughter it was necessary to say: I have permitted you to shed the blood of every living creature except your blood, which shall be forbidden to you, and to all living creatures, that they shall not have a nature to shed it."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "The citation of Bava Kamma 91a appears to refer to the passage where R. Elazar interprets this verse as a Scriptural warning against self-harm; the specific derash is cited from other sources."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:45.649150124Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}